WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.440
Hi there, it's me again.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.440
In this course, we're going to take a closer look at some system programmer concepts.

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.440
What's a system programmer? I'm so glad you asked.

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.440
That's exactly what we're going to cover here.

5
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.440
The role of a system programmer basically comes down to describing the person who installs,

6
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.440
customizes, and maintains the operating system.

7
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.440
Now, in reality, it's a bit more fuzzy than that.

8
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.440
Your typical system programmer, or SysProg,

9
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:36.440
became SysProg by having a whole lot of experiences in a number of areas all around the operating system.

10
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.440
So, what is expected of a SysProg and what a SysProg does on a day-to-day basis

11
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.440
will differ greatly from one business to the next.

12
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.440
However it's defined, the SysProg plays a central role in just about everything that has to do with their mainframe.

13
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:58.440
A system programmer needs to have knowledge of hardware, processors, system libraries and data sets,

14
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.440
storage, software, security, networking.

15
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.440
The system programmer may not be the one who actually hands-on keyboard makes those changes,

16
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.440
but they do need to understand the changes so they can help plan them,

17
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.440
minimizing impact to production workloads.

18
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.440
They typically handle building and maintaining IODF configurations,

19
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.440
architecting the connections to storage devices and other mainframes.

20
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.440
They know the system backwards and forwards, physically and logically.

21
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.440
Some typical situations a SysProg needs to be able to handle

22
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:36.440
might include selecting a performance management strategy to best optimize the mainframe performance,

23
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.440
determining when and how to expand the system.

24
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.440
You know, it's not always in the budget or the production schedule to be upgrading to the latest and greatest all the time.

25
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:51.440
A system programmer needs to be able to determine when they should roll in that new mainframe.

26
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.440
They need to establish a baseline for performance and track that performance over time,

27
00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:02.440
so problems with customers waiting too long or transactions timing out don't sneak up on them.

28
00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:08.440
A lot of what a SysProg does is making sure that a system is prepared for anticipated workloads,

29
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.440
and some of this involves working with the business side of things.

30
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.440
For example, Imagine a retail business is planning on having a huge sale in the middle of the summer

31
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.440
that's going to drive massive amounts of shoppers to their website.

32
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:27.440
The SysProg needs to know about that, and they have to have the system ready to handle that anticipated load of all those new people.

33
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.440
Perhaps most importantly, a SysProg needs to maintain a list of procedures

34
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.440
for how certain situations should be handled on their mainframe systems.

35
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:42.440
Now, I'm talking about actual written out procedures for when you see this type of problem,

36
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:49.440
look for these indicators, and based on conditions A, B, and C, perform actions D, E, or F.

37
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.440
Let me put it another way.

38
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.440
You ever hit a problem on your computer and you search for the error you get on the internet

39
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.440
and you find somebody else describing exactly the same problem,

40
00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.440
and instead of describing how they fix it, they just up and disappear,

41
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.440
or maybe they came back and their only update is,

42
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:07.440
never mind, I fixed it.

43
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.440
Procedures aren't always the most fun thing in the world to write,

44
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.440
but when you run into an issue and seconds count,

45
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.440
a well-written procedure that perfectly describes what you're seeing

46
00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.440
and what you have to do is a beautiful thing.

47
00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.440
A SysProg may not directly handle security aspects of operations.

48
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.440
They might have a dedicated security administrator for that,

49
00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.440
but the SysProg still needs to have a firm handle on who should

50
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.440
and shouldn't have access to various system resources.

51
00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.440
The reason the system programmer needs to be involved in all these areas

52
00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.440
is that there is a high expectation when it comes to enterprise systems.

53
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.440
These are not something went wrong, try again situations.

54
00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.440
In many cases, the expectation is 24 by 7 operation,

55
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.440
and often is the case that there is a contract in place guaranteeing

56
00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.440
a certain level of performance and availability.

57
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:06.440
So being reliable and performant isn't just a nice thing, it's the business.

58
00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.440
For that reason, change control is an extremely important aspect

59
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.440
of working in an enterprise environment.

60
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.440
Making changes will often require discussing what you want to do

61
00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.440
with a team of others to make sure it won't affect them

62
00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.440
or that they can anticipate them and work around those changes.

63
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.440
Even something that seems very simple and could do no harm,

64
00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.440
like upgrading a piece of software,

65
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.440
may introduce changes that affect other users.

66
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.440
Software, operating system, and hardware changes are often planned out weeks,

67
00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.440
if not months in advance.

68
00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.440
As a system programmer, this is your system.

69
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.440
You know it best, you know what it's capable of,

70
00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.440
what might put business objectives at risk,

71
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.440
and how to handle certain situations that come up.

72
00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.440
It's a tough job, but I'm pretty sure you're up to it.
